WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 190: Ronald Reagan: The Gipper, the Assassination Attempt, and Morning in America (1981–1984)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.120
[Rebecca] Today we step into the sun-drenched, optimistic world of Ronald Reagan.

4
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:19.520
[Rebecca] Armed with wit, Hollywood storytelling flair, and an unshakable faith in the American people, Reagan reshaped the modern presidency.

5
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:31.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the lifeguard from Dixon, the emergency room humor that inspired a nation, and the economic revolution of the 1980s.

6
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:53.040
[George] Ronald Wilson Reagan was born in an apartment above a bakery in Tampico, Illinois, in February 1911, the son of Jack Reagan, an Irish Catholic shoe salesman who struggled with alcoholism, and Nell Wilson Reagan, a devout, compassionate Christian woman who taught young, Dutch, to look for the good in every human soul.

7
00:00:53.040 --> 00:01:08.480
[George] Growing up in the small town of Dixon along the Rock River, young Reagan spent seven heroic summers working as a lifeguard at Lowell Park, rescuing 77 drowning swimmers from the swift currents and notching a mark on a wooden log for each life saved.

8
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:19.680
[George] Graduating from Eureka College in 1932 during the depths of the Great Depression, Reagan landed a job as a sports broadcaster at Radio Station Who in Des Moines, Iowa.

9
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.720
[George] Possessing a vivid, magical imagination.

10
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:34.960
[George] Reagan broadcast Chicago Cubs baseball games hundreds of miles away, relying solely on sparse Western Union telegraph ticker tapes, when the telegraph wire abruptly snapped in the middle of a game.

11
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:41.520
[George] Reagan smoothly ad-libbed foul balls for six minutes without missing a beat until the wire was restored.

12
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:49.520
[George] In 1937, while covering the Cubs' spring training in Southern California, he took a screen test with Warner Brothers.

13
00:01:49.520 --> 00:02:03.240
[George] Launching a 27-year Hollywood acting career in 53 feature films, forever earning the beloved nickname, The Jipper, after his iconic 1940 portrayal of doomed Notre Dame football star George Jip.

14
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:14.360
[George] Those seven summers along the Rock River taught Reagan an instinctive faith in human goodness, establishing a lifelong conviction that courage and calm vigilance could rescue anyone in danger.

15
00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:24.920
[George] The lessons of the Rock River stayed with Reagan throughout his life, that a leader must always remain calm, vigilant, and ready to extend a steady hand to anyone in trouble.

16
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.320
[George] Originally an enthusiastic New Deal Democrat who worshipped Franklin D.

17
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:39.800
[George] Roosevelt, Reagan served six terms as president of the Screen Actors Guild during the tumultuous postwar years, battling communist infiltration in the film industry.

18
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:49.720
[George] During the 1950s, Reagan was hired by General Electric as host of the weekly television program General Electric Theater and Goodwill Ambassador.

19
00:02:49.720 --> 00:03:05.720
[George] Traveling across the country for eight years visiting 135 GE manufacturing plants, Reagan spoke with hundreds of thousands of factory workers, witnessing firsthand how crushing federal regulations and high taxes strangled entrepreneurial vitality.

20
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:11.240
[George] His political transformation from liberal Democrat to conservative champion was complete.

21
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:25.400
[George] On October 27, 1964, Reagan vaulted into national political stardom by delivering a televised address on behalf of Republican presidential candidate Barry Goldwater titled, A Time for Choosing.

22
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:33.160
[George] Speaking with electric moral clarity and folksy charm, Reagan proclaimed: You and I have a rendezvous with destiny.

23
00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:38.040
[George] We will preserve for our children this, the last best hope of man on earth.

24
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:42.680
[George] Or we will sentence them to take the first step into a thousand years of darkness.

25
00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:57.120
[George] The broadcast raised an unprecedented $8 million in small donations, launching Reagan into two terms as governor of California and positioning him as the undisputed ideological leader of modern American conservatism.

26
00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:11.520
[George] That historic broadcast transformed the conservative movement from an intellectual backwater into an irresistible national crusade, demonstrating Reagan's unique gift for articulating core constitutional principles in everyday language.

27
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:22.560
[George] His speech for Goldwater demonstrated that eloquence rooted in core conviction can outlast a single electoral defeat and lay the groundwork for an enduring political transformation.

28
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:36.880
[George] Just 69 days into his presidency, on the cold, rainy afternoon of March 30, 1981, Ronald Reagan walked out of the VIP exit of the Washington Hilton Hotel after addressing an GEF LCIO convention.

29
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:54.240
[George] As Reagan smiled and waved to a crowd of reporters, 25-year-old John Hinckley J.R., seeking to impress actress Jodie Foster, stepped forward from the press cordon and fired six explosive devastator bullets from a.22 caliber revolver in 1.7 seconds.

30
00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.600
[George] Press Secretary James Brady was struck in the head.

31
00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:08.160
[George] District of Columbia police officer Thomas DeLahinty and Secret Service agent Tim McCarthy were critically wounded as McCarthy deliberately spread his body to shield the president.

32
00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:18.720
[George] Secret Service special agent in charge Jerry Parr shoved Reagan violently into the armored limousine and piled on top of him, screaming to the driver to speed to the White House.

33
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.120
[George] At first, Reagan believed Parr had broken one of his ribs in the dive.

34
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:33.480
[George] But when Reagan began coughing up bright red, frothy blood, Parr instantly changed course, ordering the limousine to George Washington University Hospital.

35
00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:49.400
[George] Unknown to anyone, the final bullet had ricocheted off the armored side panel of the limousine, flattened into a dime-sized disc, entered Reagan's left armpit, punctured his lung, and stopped less than an inch from his heart, causing massive internal bleeding.

36
00:05:49.400 --> 00:06:00.680
[George] The instant heroism of Jerry Parr and Tim McCarthy exemplified the highest devotion of the Secret Service, saving the president's life through split-second bravery and tactical discipline.

37
00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:09.880
[George] McCarthy's split second decision to take a bullet for the president became an eternal symbol of the courage and professionalism of the United States Secret Service.

38
00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:19.960
[George] Reagan walked into the emergency room under his own power, but his knees buckled and he collapsed to the tiled floor, his blood pressure plunging to 60 over 40.

39
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:31.640
[George] As medical staff frantically cut away his suit and inserted chest tubes to drain two quarts of blood, the 70-year-old president displayed an astonishing, courageous grace under pressure.

40
00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:38.360
[George] When his terrified wife Nancy rushed to his bedside, Reagan looked up at her with a gentle smile and quipped.

41
00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:45.560
[George] Honey, I forgot to duck, borrowing the immortal line boxer Jack Dempsey spoke after losing his heavyweight title.

42
00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:55.480
[George] As he was wheeled into the operating room for emergency surgery to extract the bullet, Reagan looked up through his oxygen mask at the assembled surgical team and cracked.

43
00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.040
[George] Please tell me you're all Republicans.

44
00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:58.680
[George] Dr.

45
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:06.040
[George] Joseph Giordano, a staunch liberal Democrat and chief of trauma, replied with heartfelt reverence.

46
00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:07.240
[George] Today, Mr.

47
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.360
[George] President, we are all Republicans.

48
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:16.800
[George] Unable to speak after the operation due to a breathing tube, Reagan scribbled humorous notes to his nurses.

49
00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:21.040
[George] All in all, I'd rather be in Philadelphia, and quoting W.C.

50
00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.320
[George] Fields, if I had this much attention in Hollywood, I wouldn't have left.

51
00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:33.520
[George] His courage, chivalry, and indomitable humor in the face of death united a fractured nation in awe and affection.

52
00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:44.160
[George] Reagan's unforgettable humor under fire proved to the entire world that his optimism was not a polished Hollywood act, but the genuine, unshakable core of his character.

53
00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:53.120
[George] That emergency room wit reassured millions of anxious families, showing a president whose spirit could not be conquered even in the shadow of death.

54
00:07:53.120 --> 00:08:04.320
[George] Returning to the Oval Office with renewed spiritual purpose, convinced that God had spared his life for a grand mission, Reagan unleashed an economic revolution known as Reaganomics.

55
00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:27.520
[George] Basing his policies on supply side economic theory, Reagan partnered with bipartisan congressional leaders to enact the Economic Recovery Tax Act of 1981, slashing individual income tax rates by 25% across the board, reducing the top marginal tax bracket from 70% down to 28%, and indexing tax brackets to inflation.

56
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.080
[George] In the Oval Office, business was conducted with unpretentious cheer.

57
00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:44.080
[George] A large crystal jar of jelly belly jelly beans sat permanently on his desk, which Reagan had originally used to quit pipe smoking and now offered to visiting world leaders and cabinet secretaries.

58
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:53.760
[George] When Federal Reserve Chairman Paul Volker raised interest rates to break the back of double-digit inflation, the country suffered a sharp recession in 1982.

59
00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.880
[George] Critics proclaimed Reagan's policies a failure.

60
00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.240
[George] But by late 1983, the American economy roared to life.

61
00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:16.920
[George] Inflation plummeted from 12% down to 4%, unemployment plunged, and the United States embarked upon the longest peacetime economic expansion in modern history, creating 20 million new jobs and unleashing an entrepreneurial explosion.

62
00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:29.000
[George] The economic resurgence of the mid-1980s silenced skeptics, proving that unleashing individual initiative and reducing punitive taxation could revitalize the economic engine of the republic.

63
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:39.480
[George] The economic expansion of the 1980s transformed global finance, demonstrating the extraordinary resilience and productive vitality of American free enterprise.

64
00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:45.560
[George] In 1984, Reagan ran for re-election against former vice president Walter Mondel.

65
00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:52.680
[George] The campaign's opening television commercial, created by Hal Reine, became a masterpiece of political advertising.

66
00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:54.920
[George] It's morning again in America.

67
00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:59.960
[George] Today, more men and women will go to work than ever before in our country's history.

68
00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:05.560
[George] Under the leadership of President Reagan, our country is prouder, stronger, and better.

69
00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:20.200
[George] During the first televised presidential debate in Louisville, Reagan appeared tired, hesitant, and stumbled over statistics, prompting furious media speculation that at age 73, he was too old to serve another term.

70
00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:27.880
[George] But in the second debate in Kansas City, moderator Henry Truhit asked Reagan directly if his age was a disqualifying factor.

71
00:10:27.880 --> 00:10:32.120
[George] Reagan delivered a knockout line that brought down the House with laughter.

72
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:33.160
[George] Not at all, Mr.

73
00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:38.280
[George] Truehit, and I want you to know that also I will not make age an issue of this campaign.

74
00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:44.040
[George] I am not going to exploit, for political purposes, my opponent's youth and inexperience.

75
00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:46.720
[George] Even Mondel threw his head back and laughed.

76
00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:52.960
[George] On November 6, 1984, the American electorate delivered a historic verdict.

77
00:10:52.960 --> 00:11:03.520
[George] Reagan carried 49 of 50 states, winning 525 electoral votes, the most in American history, securing 54 million popular votes.

78
00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:14.880
[George] That iconic debate reposed showcased Reagan's mastery of self-deprecating humor, turning his greatest political vulnerability into an enduring triumph with a single warm smile.

79
00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:24.640
[George] His effortless mastery of the television debate proved that genuine warmth and quick humor can dismantle partisan attacks far more effectively than angry retorts.

80
00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:32.080
[George] Reagan's extraordinary power as a leader stemmed from his profound connection to the storytelling soul of ordinary Americans.

81
00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:46.000
[George] In January 1986, when the Space Shuttle Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after liftoff, killing all seven astronauts, including schoolteacher Krista McAuliffe, Reagan cancelled his State of the Union address.

82
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:53.440
[George] Speaking directly to school children from the Oval Office, he gave an immortal eulogy penned by speechwriter Peggy Noonan.

83
00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:58.560
[George] The crew of the Space Shuttle Challenger honored us by the manner in which they lived their lives.

84
00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.160
[George] We will never forget them, nor the last time we saw them.

85
00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:09.680
[George] This morning, as they prepared for their journey and waved goodbye and slipped the surly bonds of Earth to touch the face of God.

86
00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.800
[George] Reagan never viewed himself as a great communicator.

87
00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:19.040
[George] He believed he was simply communicating great, timeless truths that belonged to the American people.

88
00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:28.240
[George] He believed that America was not merely a piece of geography, but an idea, a shining city upon a hill whose freedom was the last best hope of man.

89
00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:39.560
[George] Reagan's moving eulogy after the Challenger tragedy united a mourning country, reminding citizens that human exploration inevitably demands sacrifice in the relentless pursuit of knowledge.

90
00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:50.680
[George] The great communicator's final legacy was his profound belief that America's greatest days always lie ahead, an optimism that restored the nation's faith in its own destiny.

91
00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:59.240
[Rebecca] Ronald Reagan restored national confidence, revitalized the American economy, and survived assassination with unforgettable humor.

92
00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:04.280
[Rebecca] Yet his greatest challenge lay across the ocean in the nuclear standoff with the Soviet Union.

93
00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:20.360
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the second half of the Reagan presidency, his fiery confrontation with the evil empire, the near zero disarmament summit in Reykjavik, the Iran Contra scandal, his historic command to tear down this wall, and the peaceful end of the Cold War.

94
00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:24.440
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

